WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.085 --> 00:00:05.275
Hi, I'm Sumit Gulwani.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.640
I lead a research and engineering team

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.780
that develops APIs for programming-by-examples.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.800
These APIs are used inside various

00:00:12.805 --> 00:00:16.045
Microsoft products including Excel,

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:18.220
PowerShell, and OMS.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.280
Today, I am going to show you a demo of these APIs

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.600
in the content of a data preparation and analysis task.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.160
Consider this text file that contains 911 call records.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.660
At the top, there is some metadata

00:00:34.475 --> 00:00:36.215
and then there is a header row

00:00:37.055 --> 00:00:39.783
and then each line contains a single

00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:41.375
911 call record entry

00:00:41.935 --> 00:00:44.855
whose various fields are separated by the pipe character.

00:00:46.435 --> 00:00:49.235
Suppose I want to answer questions such as

00:00:49.235 --> 00:00:51.365
'When are vehicular accidents most common?'

00:00:52.335 --> 00:00:54.055
As in, which hour of the week?

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.360
Is it Fridays at 5 PM or Wednesdays at 4 PM?

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.220
To answer such questions, we will need to

00:01:02.225 --> 00:01:04.095
extract various fields from these records.

00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:06.995
In particular, we will need to extract this date

00:01:07.455 --> 00:01:10.215
and map it to the corresponding weekday.

00:01:10.315 --> 00:01:12.015
In this case, this is Friday.

00:01:12.675 --> 00:01:15.595
We will need to extract the time and

00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:17.605
map it to the corresponding hour bracket.

00:01:17.815 --> 00:01:19.595
In this case, this is 1 PM.

00:01:20.425 --> 00:01:22.565
And then we will need to filter the rows

00:01:22.565 --> 00:01:24.395
that correspond to vehicular accidents.

00:01:26.355 --> 00:01:29.715
So we gave this task to Python programmers in a user study

00:01:30.345 --> 00:01:32.835
and it took 30 minutes on average

00:01:32.835 --> 00:01:34.045
to do this task.

00:01:35.435 --> 00:01:38.675
Now I can do this task in 30 seconds

00:01:38.805 --> 00:01:40.315
using our APIs.

00:01:41.215 --> 00:01:44.135
This 10 to 100 productivity increase

00:01:44.135 --> 00:01:46.675
is very useful for data scientists

00:01:46.675 --> 00:01:49.955
who end up spending 80 percent of their time wrangling data.

00:01:51.205 --> 00:01:54.265
Even more interestingly, I can do this task

00:01:54.335 --> 00:01:56.895
without writing a single line of code.

00:01:58.155 --> 00:02:01.175
This is very significant since 99 percent

00:02:01.175 --> 00:02:03.815
of computer users do not know programming.

00:02:04.625 --> 00:02:07.195
And this kind of investment can bring

00:02:07.195 --> 00:02:08.705
programming to the masses.

00:02:09.935 --> 00:02:13.335
The underlying technology is called program synthesis

00:02:13.555 --> 00:02:15.405
or programming-by-examples.

00:02:16.375 --> 00:02:19.365
This is a new frontier in AI where the

00:02:19.365 --> 00:02:20.685
computer programs itself.

00:02:21.245 --> 00:02:24.675
The user simply provides input/output examples

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.180
or in some cases, simply points to the input data.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.520
And the tool comes up with an indented program

00:02:32.795 --> 00:02:34.395
for automating the task.

00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:37.575
These programs can be synthesized

00:02:37.575 --> 00:02:40.305
in different programming languages such as C#,

00:02:40.305 --> 00:02:42.085
Python, or R.

00:02:43.235 --> 00:02:45.755
Now you can watch the demo that I am going to show you

00:02:45.755 --> 00:02:47.325
by wearing one of two hats.

00:02:47.925 --> 00:02:50.725
Either you are an end user who would like to

00:02:50.725 --> 00:02:54.165
leverage these technologies for your data preparation task

00:02:54.775 --> 00:02:56.735
or you are a product developer

00:02:56.735 --> 00:02:59.645
who would like to leverage the underlying APIs

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:03.080
to develop such distinguished features for your own product

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.780
and bring the program synthesis innovation to your customers.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.200
Now the first step in analyzing this file

00:03:10.225 --> 00:03:12.855
would be to ingest it into a tabular format

00:03:13.605 --> 00:03:16.365
tools like Excel, Power BI,

00:03:16.365 --> 00:03:19.265
and SSIS allow you to do this

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.005
by specifying the various parameters such as

00:03:22.005 --> 00:03:23.580
what is a row delimiter?

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.095
What is a column delimiter?

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.920
How many lines to skip from the top?

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.520
whether or not there is a header row.

00:03:31.745 --> 00:03:34.835
Our data wrangling tool also allows you to

00:03:35.035 --> 00:03:38.035
do this by specifying various parameters.

00:03:38.975 --> 00:03:42.465
But when I point this tool to the file

00:03:42.845 --> 00:03:44.725
then it automatically analyzes

00:03:44.725 --> 00:03:47.265
the patterns in this file and is able to

00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:49.945
automatically infer the various parameters.

00:03:50.105 --> 00:03:51.205
Such as

00:03:51.895 --> 00:03:54.885
the column delimiter, which in this case is a custom

00:03:54.885 --> 00:03:57.785
separator, which is the pipe character. The fact

00:03:57.785 --> 00:04:00.445
that there are column headers, the file encoding

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:03.005
utf-16 and the fact that there

00:04:03.005 --> 00:04:04.515
are 10 lines to be skipped at the top.

00:04:06.105 --> 00:04:09.265
Now more generally, whenever you have a rich UI

00:04:09.265 --> 00:04:12.435
inside any product, you can ask the question

00:04:12.435 --> 00:04:14.095
Where the program synthesis APIs

00:04:14.095 --> 00:04:16.925
can be used to automatically predict the

00:04:16.925 --> 00:04:18.545
various parameters in that UI.

00:04:19.115 --> 00:04:22.235
Either by analyzing the underlying data

00:04:22.235 --> 00:04:24.675
or from the past user actions.

00:04:25.095 --> 00:04:27.655
So in this case, our file ingestion API

00:04:27.655 --> 00:04:30.465
is able to infer the parameters in the UI

00:04:30.465 --> 00:04:32.885
by simply analyzing the patterns in the file.

00:04:33.415 --> 00:04:34.985
And when I hit finish

00:04:36.005 --> 00:04:39.165
the inferred metadata is used to parse the file.

00:04:40.135 --> 00:04:43.485
Now let me get rid of the columns that I do not need

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:49.505
and let's look at this description column.

00:04:50.615 --> 00:04:53.325
It contains various soft fields that I am interested in

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:56.425
such as date and time.

00:04:57.525 --> 00:05:00.285
Splitting this long string into the various constraining

00:05:00.285 --> 00:05:02.415
fields is not easy

00:05:02.415 --> 00:05:04.835
because there are multiple delimiters such as the

00:05:04.835 --> 00:05:08.205
hyphen character, the @ character

00:05:08.205 --> 00:05:09.985
the semicolon character

00:05:10.325 --> 00:05:13.215
and even more challenging is the fact that not all

00:05:13.215 --> 00:05:16.265
occurrences of hyphens are delimiters, such as the ones

00:05:16.265 --> 00:05:17.585
that occur inside the date.

00:05:19.495 --> 00:05:21.895
Now in order to split such a string

00:05:21.895 --> 00:05:24.775
into various constraining fields, we have developed an API

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.820
to split columns by example.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.205
Now when I invoke this API it also tries

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:32.620
to behave predictively.

00:05:32.825 --> 00:05:35.545
Meaning it first tries to split the column

00:05:35.545 --> 00:05:38.305
without any examples and then I can

00:05:38.305 --> 00:05:41.205
provide the examples at the places where it got it wrong.

00:05:42.195 --> 00:05:44.805
So in this case, it extracted the

00:05:44.885 --> 00:05:47.985
first column correctly and the second column correctly

00:05:48.155 --> 00:05:50.375
but in the third column, there are some

00:05:50.375 --> 00:05:51.805
errors in row three

00:05:52.415 --> 00:05:54.715
and row twenty-one and so on.

00:05:56.405 --> 00:05:59.445
So now manually browsing for such errors

00:05:59.445 --> 00:06:02.355
can also be quite tedious, especially when the dataset

00:06:02.355 --> 00:06:05.595
is large and the errors do not occur in your viewport.

00:06:07.025 --> 00:06:09.075
So when I go to the advanced mode

00:06:09.795 --> 00:06:12.285
the interactive version of this API

00:06:12.475 --> 00:06:15.515
actually points out all the six different formats that

00:06:15.515 --> 00:06:16.585
occur in the dataset

00:06:16.935 --> 00:06:20.045
and I can quickly inspect the output on the six formats

00:06:20.075 --> 00:06:22.125
to identify where the errors are.

00:06:22.785 --> 00:06:25.605
And in this case, I give an example

00:06:25.765 --> 00:06:27.705
to fix the time column

00:06:28.215 --> 00:06:30.165
and when I hit the apply button

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.900
the tool tries to learn a program

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.360
which is consistent with the example that I provided.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.720
And then when I hit okay

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.480
the tool executes this program on the rest of the dataset

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.340
to produce the intended splitting.

00:06:46.375 --> 00:06:48.215
Now the next step in this task

00:06:48.215 --> 00:06:50.115
is to do some transformations

00:06:50.965 --> 00:06:53.155
such as map the date

00:06:53.155 --> 00:06:55.125
to the corresponding weekday.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:00.720
Now I can do this using the

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.460
Derive Column by Example capability that we have developed

00:07:04.205 --> 00:07:07.045
and all that I need to do is to give an example of

00:07:07.255 --> 00:07:09.245
the transformation that I want to do.

00:07:09.885 --> 00:07:12.745
So when I type Friday for this corresponding date

00:07:12.745 --> 00:07:15.105
know that the autocomplete feature of the tool

00:07:15.105 --> 00:07:17.725
already provides me with the

00:07:18.255 --> 00:07:19.795
completed example

00:07:21.045 --> 00:07:24.305
and when I hit enter the tool tries to learn a program

00:07:24.305 --> 00:07:26.905
which is consistent with the example that I provided.

00:07:30.595 --> 00:07:33.125
So I will hit okay to accept the changes.

00:07:34.305 --> 00:07:37.495
Now if you look on the right side the tool has started building

00:07:37.495 --> 00:07:40.065
the entire script as a series of steps.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.700
If I hover over this last step that I did

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:46.120
the tool actually shows me what kind of

00:07:46.125 --> 00:07:49.015
transformation it inferred. So in particular it says

00:07:49.015 --> 00:07:51.695
that the transformation is to extract the day

00:07:51.695 --> 00:07:53.615
of the week from the date time.

00:07:54.165 --> 00:07:57.445
So now we would like to map the time column

00:07:57.445 --> 00:07:59.025
to the corresponding bucket

00:07:59.495 --> 00:08:01.035
and I can again do this

00:08:01.385 --> 00:08:03.045
by giving an example.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.140
So suppose I want to map it to the

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.580
corresponding half an hour bucket

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.120
all that I would do is to give an example.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.020
For those of you who are familiar with the flash fill feature

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.540
in Microsoft Excel, this capability

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.520
is much more advanced than that since this can manipulate

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.120
dates, numbers, times.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.200
Now instead of the half an hour bucket

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.620
suppose I want to map it to the corresponding two hour bucket.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.260
Again I can do this

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.600
by means of simply providing an example.

00:08:36.145 --> 00:08:39.145
Now just imagine how much effort would it be

00:08:39.145 --> 00:08:42.565
if you were to do this by writing a program yourself.

00:08:43.965 --> 00:08:47.145
But in this case I simply wanted the corresponding hour.

00:08:47.145 --> 00:08:49.725
So 1 PM suffices for me here

00:08:50.075 --> 00:08:52.455
and the system is able to learn the transformation

00:08:52.455 --> 00:08:53.955
with again one example.

00:08:55.165 --> 00:08:57.975
Now initially I promised you that I would be able

00:08:57.975 --> 00:09:00.625
to do this demo in 30 seconds

00:09:00.625 --> 00:09:02.475
but I have gone much more than that.

00:09:03.075 --> 00:09:06.195
So turns out that all these transformations that I did

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.140
I didn't really need to do them.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:11.540
I was doing them just to show you the various capabilities.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.940
So if you recall, within 10 seconds

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.755
I was at this screen where I was able to automatically ingest

00:09:18.755 --> 00:09:21.005
the file into a tabular format

00:09:21.415 --> 00:09:24.395
and then this description column contains

00:09:24.395 --> 00:09:26.555
the relevant fields that I am interested in.

00:09:26.945 --> 00:09:29.945
If all that I wanted to do was to map it

00:09:29.945 --> 00:09:32.295
to the corresponding week hour

00:09:32.485 --> 00:09:35.505
I can do that simply by giving an example

00:09:35.505 --> 00:09:37.335
instead of having to split it

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.560
into the various constraining fields.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.340
And now the last step in the demo

00:09:44.345 --> 00:09:47.335
is to filter the rows in the dataset

00:09:47.335 --> 00:09:50.235
to those that correspond to vehicular accident.

00:09:50.975 --> 00:09:53.475
I can do that by invoking the

00:09:53.475 --> 00:09:55.005
filter column feature.

00:09:56.765 --> 00:09:59.935
And maybe I want to use a predicate which says

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.280
ends with vehicular accident.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.240
The moment I type 'v'

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.160
the autocomplete variations pop up

00:10:07.975 --> 00:10:10.065
and it not only avoids me

00:10:10.065 --> 00:10:13.075
having to type multiple keystrokes but you can also

00:10:13.075 --> 00:10:16.365
see that it tells me what percentage of the rows would match if I were to pick

00:10:16.365 --> 00:10:17.745
a certain edition.

00:10:18.365 --> 00:10:21.245
But most significantly it shows me

00:10:21.245 --> 00:10:23.135
that I was about to do a wrong computation

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.200
without probably even realizing it

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.160
because there are certain rows in the dataset that end with vehicular accident

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.500
without a hyphen.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.880
Now if you are a

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.160
Python programmer and you did not notice this thing

00:10:34.165 --> 00:10:36.835
you could have computed the wrong result without realizing it.

00:10:37.015 --> 00:10:40.195
So instead what I want to use is the predicate

00:10:40.575 --> 00:10:43.685
which says contains vehicular accident as opposed to

00:10:43.685 --> 00:10:45.335
ends with vehicular accident.

00:10:47.245 --> 00:10:50.675
And when I do that the system then ends up

00:10:50.735 --> 00:10:52.695
filtering the rows to the

00:10:52.695 --> 00:10:54.465
ones that correspond to vehicular accident.

00:10:54.835 --> 00:10:58.095
And all that I have to do now is to simply count

00:10:58.175 --> 00:11:01.665
the number of different week hours here.

00:11:01.725 --> 00:11:04.635
And now we notice that most vehicular accidents

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.080
happen on Thursdays at 5 PM

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.000
and in the rush hour traffic of the Wednesdays are the worst hour.

00:11:10.225 --> 00:11:12.985
So you see in 30 seconds you are able to draw

00:11:12.985 --> 00:11:15.215
some very good insights from this data

00:11:15.215 --> 00:11:17.505
and now you know when to avoid driving.

00:11:18.375 --> 00:11:20.615
If you want to do this demo yourself

00:11:20.615 --> 00:11:21.955
send me email.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.460
Also the underlying APIs

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:28.220
are hosted on GitHub with appropriate documentation and samples.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.245
If you would like to use these APIs

00:11:30.245 --> 00:11:32.845
inside your own product please get in touch

00:11:32.845 --> 00:11:35.395
and we would love to engage with you. Thank you.

